(Answer: Ideal characteristics: \(1\) Infinite open-loop differential gain A_vd = inf, \(2\)) Tj
T*
(Infinite input impedance R_in = inf \(zero input current\), \(3\) Zero output impedance) Tj
T*
(R_out = 0, \(4\) Infinite bandwidth, \(5\) Infinite CMRR, \(6\) Infinite Slew Rate, \(7\) Zero) Tj
T*
(offset voltage. Concept of Virtual Ground: When negative feedback is applied, the high) Tj
T*
(open-loop gain ensures the output voltage remains within supply boundaries: V_out = A_vd) Tj
T*
(* \(V+ - V-\). Since A_vd -> inf, differential input voltage must be virtually zero: V+ -) Tj
T*
(V- = V_out / A_vd = 0 => V- = V+. If the non-inverting terminal \(+\) is tied to ground) Tj
T*
(\(V+ = 0\), the inverting terminal \(-\) is maintained at 0V potential. It acts as a ground) Tj
T*
(for all circuit calculations, yet no physical path exists to ground \(no current flows) Tj
T*
(into the pin due to infinite input impedance\).) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Explain the operation and frequency response limitations of an Op-Amp) Tj
T*
(differentiator, and describe how practical differentiators overcome instability.) Tj
T*
(Answer: An ideal differentiator has input capacitor C and feedback resistor R, yielding) Tj
T*
(V_out = - R*C*\(dV_in/dt\). Its transfer function is H\(s\) = - s*R*C, which has a magnitude) Tj
T*
(|H\(j*omega\)| = omega*R*C that increases linearly with frequency at +20 dB/decade without) Tj
T*
(limit. This causes two severe practical problems: \(1\) High-frequency electrical noise is) Tj
T*
(drastically magnified, obscuring desired signals, \(2\) Phase lag causes closed-loop) Tj
T*
(instability and high-frequency oscillations. A practical differentiator adds a small) Tj
T*
(series resistor R_s at the input and a small parallel capacitor C_f across feedback) Tj
T*
(resistor R. At high frequencies, R_s and C_f attenuate signals, converting the circuit) Tj
T*
(into an integrator/low-pass filter above a critical corner frequency f_c = 1 /) Tj
T*
(\(2*pi*R_s*C\), stabilizing the loop.) Tj
